{"abstract":"OBJECTIVES: To investigate the association between contemporary combined hormonal contraceptives (including progestogen types in combined preparations and all progestogen-only products) and overall and specific types of ovarian cancer. DESIGN: Prospective, nationwide cohort study. SETTING: Denmark, 1995-2014. PARTICIPANTS: All women aged 15-49 years during 1995-2014 were eligible. Women were excluded if they immigrated after 1995, had cancer (except non-melanoma skin cancer), had venous thrombosis, or were treated for infertility before entry (final study population included 1 879 227 women). Women were categorised as never users (no record of being dispensed hormonal contraception), current or recent users (≤1 year after stopping use), or former users (>1 year after stopping use) of different hormonal contraceptives. MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES: Poisson regression was used to calculate relative risk of ovarian cancer among users of any contemporary combined hormonal contraceptives and by progestogen type in combined preparations and all progestogen-only products, including non-oral preparations. Separate analyses examined women followed up to their first contraception type switch and those with full contraceptive histories. Duration, time since last use, and tumour histology were examined and the population prevented fraction were calculated. RESULTS: During 21.4 million person years, 1249 incident ovarian cancers occurred. Among ever users of hormonal contraception, 478 ovarian cancers were recorded over 13 344 531 person years. Never users had 771 ovarian cancers during 8 150 250 person years. Compared with never users, reduced risks of ovarian cancer occurred with current or recent use and former use of any hormonal contraception (relative risk 0.58 (95% confidence interval 0.49 to 0.68) and 0.77 (0.66 to 0.91), respectively). Relative risks among current or recent users decreased with increasing duration (from 0.82 (0.59 to 1.12) with ≤1 year use to 0.26 (0.16 to 0.43) with >10 years' use; P<0.001 for trend). Similar results were achieved among women followed up to their first switch in contraceptive type. Little evidence of major differences in risk estimates by tumour type or progestogen content of combined oral contraceptives was seen. Use of progestogen-only products were not associated with ovarian cancer risk. Among ever users of hormonal contraception, the reduction in the age standardised absolute rate of ovarian cancer was 3.2 per 100 000 person years. Based on the relative risk for the never use versus ever use categories of hormonal contraception (0.66), the population prevented fraction was estimated to be 21%-that is, use of hormonal contraception prevented 21% of ovarian cancers in the study population. CONCLUSIONS: Use of contemporary combined hormonal contraceptives is associated with a reduction in ovarian cancer risk in women of reproductive age-an effect related to duration of use, which diminishes after stopping use. These data suggest no protective effect from progestogen-only products.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Iversen, Lisa","Fielding, Shona","Lidegaard, Øjvind","Mørch, Lina Steinrud","Skovlund, Charlotte W","Hannaford, Philip C"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-532223"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Aberdeen"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Aged","Cohort Studies","Contraceptives Oral Combined adverse effects","Contraceptives Oral Hormonal adverse effects","Denmark epidemiology","Female","Humans","Incidence","Middle Aged","Progestins adverse effects","Prospective Studies","Risk Factors","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"association between contemporary hormonal contraception and ovarian cancer in women of reproductive age in denmark prospective nationwide cohort study","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.k3609","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-532223","identifier":[{"id":"85054177252","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC6283376","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"30257920","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-e8671b38-e015-48f4-a854-33bf4f0222c5"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Aged","Cohort Studies","Contraception/adverse effects","Contraceptives, Oral, Combined/adverse effects","Contraceptives, Oral, Hormonal/adverse effects","Denmark/epidemiology","Female","Humans","Incidence","Middle Aged","Ovarian Neoplasms/chemically induced","Progestins/adverse effects","Prospective Studies","Risk Factors","Young Adult"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BAF3B85364AE4972DC5E6F6F1163D6E4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/222165225/bmj.k3609.full.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.k3609","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of aberdeen","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Aberdeen","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Lisa","key":"iversenpau","last":"Iversen","name":"Iversen, Lisa","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Shona","key":"fieldingpau","last":"Fielding","name":"Fielding, Shona","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Øjvind","key":"lidegaardpau","last":"Lidegaard","locid":"477519a8-a5d9-4dcf-970a-e11c7e4f936f","name":"Lidegaard, Øjvind","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Lina S","key":"mørchpau","last":"Mørch","locid":"e90113de-192f-4240-9083-e3a32a1998f1","name":"Mørch, Lina Steinrud","name_org":"Mørch, Lina S","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Charlotte W","key":"skovlundpau","last":"Skovlund","name":"Skovlund, Charlotte W","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Philip C","key":"hannafordpau","last":"Hannaford","name":"Hannaford, Philip C","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30257920"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1136/bmj.k3609","issn":["09598146","17561833"],"issn_org":["09598146"],"journal":"BMJ","pages":"1-9","paperid":"k3609","status":"p","title":"BMJ","title_org":"BMJ (Clinical research ed.)","volume":"362"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85054177252"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-e8671b38-e015-48f4-a854-33bf4f0222c5"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Association between contemporary hormonal contraception and ovarian cancer in women of reproductive age in Denmark : prospective, nationwide cohort study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-532223","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"IMPORTANCE: The association between the use of hormonal contraceptive and pancreatic cancer among premenopausal women has until now been unclear. This is the first study to investigate the risk of pancreatic cancer in pre-menopausal women. OBJECTIVE: To determine whether hormonal contraception increases the risk of developing pancreatic cancer in pre-menopausal women. DESIGN: A nationwide prospective cohort study followed all women in Denmark in the age range of 15-49 years without previous cancer or venous thrombosis from 1995 to 2014. The Danish National Prescription Registry provided individually updated exposure information on use of hormonal contraception. The Danish Cancer Registry provided cancer diagnoses, and the Danish National Patient Register containing clinical diagnoses and surgical codes at discharge from public and private hospitals. SETTING: Population-based cohort study. PARTICIPANTS: All women living in Denmark aged 15-49 years at January 1st, 1995, and those subsequently reaching age 15 years up to December 31st, 2014 were eligible for the study. RESULTS: Among 1.9 million women who were followed on average for 11.4 years, 235 pancreatic cancers occurred. Compared to never users, ever users of any type of hormonal contraception had a relative risk (RR) of pancreatic cancer of 0.90 (95% confidence interval (CI) 0.68-1.19). No overall association between duration of hormonal contraceptive use and pancreatic cancer risk was found. Neither was long-term use of hormonal contraception associated with pancreas cancer, RR 0.83 (95% CI 0.47-1.50). The risk did not vary between users of combined and progestogen-only products. All models were adjusted for age, completed or ongoing education, polycystic ovary syndrome, endometriosis and among parous women; parity, age at first birth, smoking and body mass index. CONCLUSIONS AND RELEVANCE: Compared to never users the risk of pancreatic cancer is not significantly higher among current and recent users of contemporary hormonal contraception and does not vary between users of combined and progestogen-only products. In conclusion, our study suggests no risk of pancreatic cancer with use of any type of hormonal contraception.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Butt, Sedrah Arif","Lidegaard, Øjvind","Skovlund, Charlotte","Hannaford, Philip C","Iversen, Lisa","Fielding, Shona","Mørch, Lina Steinrud"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-515937"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Body Mass Index","Cohort Studies","Contraceptives Oral Hormonal adverse effects","Denmark epidemiology","Endometriosis complications","Female","Humans","Middle Aged","Pancreatic Neoplasms diagnosis","Polycystic Ovary Syndrome complications","Premenopause","Prospective Studies","Registries","Risk","Smoking","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hormonal contraceptive use and risk of pancreatic cancer a cohort study among premenopausal women","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0206358","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-515937","identifier":[{"id":"30376560","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85055665963","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC6207333","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-cd185b85-c00e-485b-b80b-1c6fb3756376"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Body Mass Index","Cohort Studies","Contraceptives, Oral, Hormonal/adverse effects","Denmark/epidemiology","Endometriosis/complications","Female","Humans","Middle Aged","Pancreatic Neoplasms/diagnosis","Polycystic Ovary Syndrome/complications","Premenopause","Prospective Studies","Registries","Risk","Smoking","Young Adult"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-320C4E49CC27D44710584CA7CB236CF5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0206358","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/216567251/journal.pone.0206358.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Sedrah Arif","key":"buttpau","last":"Butt","name":"Butt, Sedrah Arif","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Øjvind","key":"lidegaardipau","last":"Lidegaardi","locid":"477519a8-a5d9-4dcf-970a-e11c7e4f936f","name":"Lidegaard, Øjvind","name_org":"Lidegaardi, Øjvind","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Charlotte","key":"skovlundpau","last":"Skovlund","name":"Skovlund, Charlotte","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Philip C","key":"hannafordpau","last":"Hannaford","name":"Hannaford, Philip C","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lisa","key":"iversenpau","last":"Iversen","name":"Iversen, Lisa","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Shona","key":"fieldingpau","last":"Fielding","name":"Fielding, Shona","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lina Steinrud","key":"mørchpau","last":"Mørch","name":"Mørch, Lina Steinrud","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30376560"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0206358","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"10","journal":"PLOS ONE","paperid":"e0206358","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85055665963"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-cd185b85-c00e-485b-b80b-1c6fb3756376"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Hormonal contraceptive use and risk of pancreatic cancer : A cohort study among premenopausal women","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-515937","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Background: Patients suffering from depression have a high risk of relapse and readmission in the weeks following discharge from inpatient wards. Electronic self-monitoring systems that offer patient-communication features are now available to offer daily support to patients, but the usability, acceptability, and adherence to these systems has only been sparsely investigated. Objective: We aim to test the usability, acceptability, adherence, and clinical outcome of a newly developed computer-based electronic self-assessment system (the Daybuilder system) in patients suffering from depression, in the period from discharge until commencing outpatient treatment in the Intensive Outpatient Unit for Affective Disorders. Methods: Patients suffering from unipolar major depression that were referred from inpatient wards to an intensive outpatient unit were included in this study before their discharge, and were followed for four weeks. User satisfaction was assessed using semiqualitative questionnaires and the System Usability Scale (SUS). Patients were interviewed at baseline and at endpoint with the Hamilton depression rating scale (HAM-D17), the Major Depression Inventory (MDI), and the 5-item World Health Organization Well-Being Index (WHO-5). In this four-week period patients used the Daybuilder system to self-monitor mood, sleep, activity, and medication adherence on a daily basis. The system displayed a graphical representation of the data that was simultaneously displayed to patients and clinicians. Patients were phoned weekly to discuss their data entries. The primary outcomes were usability, acceptability, and adherence to the system. The secondary outcomes were changes in: the electronically self-assessed mood, sleep, and activity scores; and scores from the HAM-D17, MDI, and WHO-5 scales. Results: In total, 76% of enrolled patients (34/45) completed the four-week study. Five patients were readmitted due to relapse. The 34 patients that completed the study entered data for mood on 93.8% of the days (872/930), sleep on 89.8% of the days (835/930), activity on 85.6% of the days (796/930), and medication on 88.0 % of the days (818/930). SUS scores were 86.2 (standard deviation [SD] 9.7) and 79% of the patients (27/34) found that the system lived up to their expectations. A significant improvement in depression severity was found on the HAM-D17 from 18.0 (SD 6.5) to 13.3 (SD 7.3; P<.01), on the MDI from 27.1 (SD 13.1) to 22.1 (SD 12.7; P=.006), and in quality of life on the WHO-5 from 31.3 (SD 22.9) to 43.4 (SD 22.1; P<.001) scales, but not on self-assessed mood (P=.08). Mood and sleep parameters were highly variable from day-to-day. Sleep-offset was significantly delayed from baseline, averaging 48 minutes (standard error 12 minutes; P<.001). Furthermore, when estimating delay of sleep-onset (with sleep quality included in the model) during the study period, this showed a significant negative effect on mood (P=.03) Conclusions: The Daybuilder systems performed well technically, and patients were satisfied with the system and had high adherence to self-assessments. The dropout rate and the gradual delay in sleep emphasize the need for continued clinical support for these patients, especially when considering sleep guidance.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Lauritsen, Lise","Andersen, Louise","Olsson, Emilia","Søndergaard, Stine Rauff","Nørregaard, Lasse Benn","Løventoft, Philip Kaare","Svendsen, Signe Dunker","Frøkjær, Erik","Jensen, Hans Mørch","Hageman, Ida","Kessing, Lars Vedel","Martiny, Klaus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-539071"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Chronotherapeutics","Depressive Disorder","Electronic monitoring","Graph drawing","Inpatients","Major","Observational Study","Patient Participation","Self Assessment","Sleep"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"usability acceptability and adherence to an electronic self monitoring system in patients with major depression discharged from inpatient wards","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/jmir.6673","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-539071","identifier":[{"id":"PMC5420067","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85018764893","type":"scopus"},{"id":"28432040","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-f3f843aa-ac4b-4c2e-a5f6-4e6a78f5af5f"],"keyword":["Chronotherapeutics","Depressive disorder","Electronic monitoring","Graph drawing","Inpatients","Major","Observational study","Patient participation","Self-assessment","Sleep"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8C7C2B44C1FD5190497BD7A2BE710E65"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/jmir.6673","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/179556825/Lauritsen_2017_Usability_acceptability.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Mental Health Services Glostrup"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Monsenso ApS"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Lise","key":"lauritsenpau","last":"Lauritsen","name":"Lauritsen, Lise","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Louise","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","name":"Andersen, Louise","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Emilia","key":"olssonpau","last":"Olsson","name":"Olsson, Emilia","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Stine Rauff","key":"søndergaardpau","last":"Søndergaard","name":"Søndergaard, Stine Rauff","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Lasse Benn","key":"nørregaardpau","last":"Nørregaard","name":"Nørregaard, Lasse Benn","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Philip Kaare","key":"løventoftpau","last":"Løventoft","name":"Løventoft, Philip Kaare","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Signe Dunker","key":"svendsenpau","last":"Svendsen","name":"Svendsen, Signe Dunker","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Erik","key":"frøkjærpau","last":"Frøkjær","locid":"93f6716d-45dd-407f-800c-f5b7bc9286ff","name":"Frøkjær, Erik","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hans Mørch","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","name":"Jensen, Hans Mørch","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ida Hageman","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"c5a125b7-2e59-44d2-a6b3-964d2ea251e3","name":"Hageman, Ida","name_org":"Pedersen, Ida Hageman","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lars Vedel","key":"kessingpau","last":"Kessing","locid":"93a97a97-e9d8-464b-8eed-1244a0a9ee56","name":"Kessing, Lars Vedel","orcid":"0000-0001-9377-9436","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Klaus","key":"martinypau","last":"Martiny","locid":"756e4617-fb07-492d-83e7-48cd00832346","name":"Martiny, Klaus","orcid":"0000-0002-7317-5958","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["28432040"],"pub":{"doi":"10.2196/jmir.6673","issn":["14388871","14394456"],"issn_org":["14394456"],"issue":"4","journal":"Journal of Medical Internet Research","paperid":"e123","status":"p","title":"Journal of Medical Internet Research","volume":"19"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85018764893"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Private Research","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Other Companies","Private Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen","monsenso aps","rehfeld medical aps (biz)"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-f3f843aa-ac4b-4c2e-a5f6-4e6a78f5af5f"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Usability, acceptability, and adherence to an electronic self-monitoring system in patients with major depression discharged from inpatient wards","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-539071","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Little is known about whether contemporary hormonal contraception is associated with an increased risk of breast cancer. METHODS: We assessed associations between the use of hormonal contraception and the risk of invasive breast cancer in a nationwide prospective cohort study involving all women in Denmark between 15 and 49 years of age who had not had cancer or venous thromboembolism and who had not received treatment for infertility. Nationwide registries provided individually updated information about the use of hormonal contraception, breast-cancer diagnoses, and potential confounders. RESULTS: Among 1.8 million women who were followed on average for 10.9 years (a total of 19.6 million person-years), 11,517 cases of breast cancer occurred. As compared with women who had never used hormonal contraception, the relative risk of breast cancer among all current and recent users of hormonal contraception was 1.20 (95% confidence interval [CI], 1.14 to 1.26). This risk increased from 1.09 (95% CI, 0.96 to 1.23) with less than 1 year of use to 1.38 (95% CI, 1.26 to 1.51) with more than 10 years of use (P=0.002). After discontinuation of hormonal contraception, the risk of breast cancer was still higher among the women who had used hormonal contraceptives for 5 years or more than among women who had not used hormonal contraceptives. Risk estimates associated with current or recent use of various oral combination (estrogen-progestin) contraceptives varied between 1.0 and 1.6. Women who currently or recently used the progestin-only intrauterine system also had a higher risk of breast cancer than women who had never used hormonal contraceptives (relative risk, 1.21; 95% CI, 1.11 to 1.33). The overall absolute increase in breast cancers diagnosed among current and recent users of any hormonal contraceptive was 13 (95% CI, 10 to 16) per 100,000 person-years, or approximately 1 extra breast cancer for every 7690 women using hormonal contraception for 1 year. CONCLUSIONS: The risk of breast cancer was higher among women who currently or recently used contemporary hormonal contraceptives than among women who had never used hormonal contraceptives, and this risk increased with longer durations of use; however, absolute increases in risk were small. (Funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation.).","author_count":6,"contributors":["Mørch, Lina S","Skovlund, Charlotte W","Hannaford, Philip C","Iversen, Lisa","Fielding, Shona","Lidegaard, Øjvind"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-467308"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Age Distribution","Breast Neoplasms chemically induced","Contraceptives Oral Hormonal adverse effects","Denmark epidemiology","Estrogens adverse effects","Female","Humans","Intrauterine Devices Medicated adverse effects","Progestins adverse effects","Prospective Studies","Registries","Risk","Risk Assessment","Time Factors","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"contemporary hormonal contraception and the risk of breast cancer","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-467308","identifier":[{"id":"29211679","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85038233522","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-7b5af21b-3ccb-434d-b2b6-7a135d6164c5"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Age Distribution","Breast Neoplasms/chemically induced","Contraceptives, Oral, Hormonal/adverse effects","Denmark/epidemiology","Estradiol/adverse effects","Estrogens/adverse effects","Female","Humans","Intrauterine Devices, Medicated/adverse effects","Progestins/adverse effects","Prospective Studies","Registries","Risk","Risk Assessment","Time Factors","Young Adult"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-4-93EC5595167767784700DC1E55D80C24"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Lina S","key":"mørchpau","last":"Mørch","name":"Mørch, Lina S","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Charlotte W","key":"skovlundpau","last":"Skovlund","name":"Skovlund, Charlotte W","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Philip C","key":"hannafordpau","last":"Hannaford","name":"Hannaford, Philip C","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lisa","key":"iversenpau","last":"Iversen","name":"Iversen, Lisa","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Shona","key":"fieldingpau","last":"Fielding","name":"Fielding, Shona","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Øjvind","key":"lidegaardpau","last":"Lidegaard","locid":"477519a8-a5d9-4dcf-970a-e11c7e4f936f","name":"Lidegaard, Øjvind","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29211679"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1056/nejmoa1700732","issn":["00284793","15334406"],"issn_org":["00284793"],"issue":"23","journal":"The New England Journal of Medicine","pages":"2228-2239","status":"p","title":"The New England Journal of Medicine","volume":"377"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85038233522"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-7b5af21b-3ccb-434d-b2b6-7a135d6164c5"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Contemporary Hormonal Contraception and the Risk of Breast Cancer","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-467308","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
